mode: "100644"
text: "# Edit passes, the tone map & worked examples\n\n## The four-pass edit (run in order on any draft)\n1. SUBSTANCE: mark every sentence anyone could have written → replace or cut; insert one only-us specific.\n2. WEIGHT: delete fluff sentences whole; cut throat-clearing; verbs over adjectives; front-load.\n3. RHYTHM: vary sentence lengths (long-build → short landing); read aloud; fix every stumble.\n4. TELLS: sweep the cluster (delve-class vocabulary, \"It's not X it's Y\", forced threes, rigid shape,\n   misplaced social proof, relentless positivity) — with judgment, never bans.\n\n## The tone map (one voice, rendered per moment)\n| Moment | Register | The playful-brand example |\n|---|---|---|\n| Celebration | full personality | \"We did the thing. The thing is DONE.\" |\n| Education | warm, clear | \"Here's the part everyone skips — don't.\" |\n| Sales | confident, restrained | \"It works. Here's the proof; you decide.\" |\n| Complaint reply | human, accountable | \"You're right, we got this wrong. Fixed by Friday.\" |\n| Crisis / bad news | plain, direct | \"We're reducing the team by 12 people. Here's why, and what they receive.\" |\nVoice fingerprints persist; cleverness dials to zero as stakes rise.\n\n## Before/after (the tells, fixed with substance)\nBEFORE: \"In today's fast-paced world, it's not about working harder — it's about working smarter. First,\nprioritize. Second, delegate. Third, automate. This is a testament to the power of leverage.\"\nAFTER: \"I tracked my week once: 11 hours went to tasks a €40 tool now does. Working 'smarter' meant admitting\na machine files receipts better than I do. My Thursday afternoons came back.\"\n(What changed: owned specifics replaced the parallel constructions, the forced three, and the hallmark words —\nnothing was 'hidden'; something real was said.)\n\n## The read-aloud protocol\nPrint or full-screen the draft → read aloud at speaking pace → mark every stumble, breath-gasp, and eye-skip →\neach mark is an edit (shorten, split, or cut) → re-read. Two passes maximum; then ship or escalate to a human\neditor.\n\n## Worked example A — \"Blunt indie founder\" (the substance fix)\n\"a reader commented 'nice chatgpt post' on something i actually wrote myself. rude, but fair — i'd written it\nlike a press release. rewrote it with the actual number (we lost 3 clients in march), the actual mistake\n(mine), and one opinion i'd defend at dinner. same topic. nobody asks who wrote my posts anymore, because\nthey sound like a person who was there.\" — the tells were symptoms; specificity was the cure.\n\n## Worked example B — \"Warm bookkeeping studio\" (the tone map in a hard week)\n\"Our voice is gentle and a little playful — but the week we wrote about a client's bankruptcy, playful would\nhave been grotesque. We kept our words simple and our sentences short, and readers said it was the most\n'us' thing we'd published. One small step: write down how your brand sounds on its best day, then decide —\nbefore you need it — how it sounds on its hardest one.\" — voice constant, tone chosen deliberately.\n\n## Never\nInvent personal anecdotes, failures, or credentials · stage typos or fake imperfection as 'humanity' · chase\ndetector scores · ban punctuation as policy · ship a fluff sentence · publish without the read-aloud pass ·\nlet platform-native voices erase the brand's · use cleverness in a crisis · fabricate the human's experiences\nor feelings · skip AI-disclosure where required.\n"
